<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">World Trade Organization</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as WTO</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The World Trade Organization is an international body founded in 1995 to succeed the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade and regulate trade between its members. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s entry in 2001 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s in 2007 marked their integration into the world economy after decades of market reform.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/world-trade-organization.jpg" alt="World Trade Organization" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Happenstance et al /; derivative work by Danlaycock · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WTO_members_and_observers.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade was set up by a multilateral treaty of 23 countries after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, alongside other new institutions for economic cooperation such as the World Bank and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a>. A proposed International Trade Organization never started, because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and other signatories did not ratify its treaty, so the GATT slowly became a de facto international organisation, operating for almost half a century as a provisional, semi-institutionalised treaty regime. Seven rounds of negotiation between 1949 and 1979 first cut tariffs. The Kennedy Round of the mid-sixties produced an anti-dumping agreement, and the Tokyo Round of the seventies made the first major attempt to tackle barriers that were not tariffs.</p><p>The eighth round, the Uruguay Round, was launched at Punta del Este in September 1986. It extended the system to services and intellectual property and reformed trade in agriculture and textiles. Its Final Act, the Marrakesh Agreement, was signed on 15 April 1994, and the WTO was established on 1 January 1995, with its headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The WTO provides a framework for negotiating trade agreements and for settling disputes among its members. Its agreements cover goods, services and intellectual property, and rest on non-discrimination, expressed in the most-favoured-nation and national treatment provisions, with exceptions for purposes such as environmental protection and national security. Its highest body, the Ministerial Conference, usually meets every two years and decides by consensus. The General Council, made up of representatives of all members, handles day-to-day business, and a Secretariat led by a Director-General administers the organisation.</p><p>Unlike the GATT, whose rules were ambiguous and which let members block dispute settlement, the WTO has more numerous and more precise obligations, and members that do not comply may face punishment. Its consensus rule has also contributed to deadlock. Ministerial conferences have sometimes been contentious: the Seattle conference of 1999 provoked large demonstrations, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doha">Doha</a> in 2001 approved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s entry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Doha Development Round, launched in November 2001, aimed to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/globalisation">globalisation</a> more inclusive and to cut barriers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> in farming. Talks stalled over differences between developed nations and major low-income and lower-middle-income countries, and over agricultural subsidies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> conference agreed to phase out agricultural export subsidies, and the Bali Ministerial Declaration of 2013 addressed bureaucratic barriers to commerce. Economic studies generally find that the WTO has boosted trade and reduced trade barriers. Critics argue that the benefits of free trade are not shared equally, that its agreements may disadvantage developing countries and that commercial interests have been put before environmental and labour concerns.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “World Trade Organization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>China joined the <b>World Trade Organization</b> in December 2001, hosted the Summer Olympic Games in Beijing in 2008 and passed Japan as the world’s second-largest economy in 2010, and around 2011 its urban population overtook the rural.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Cold War, Vietnam normalised relations with China in 1991 and with the United States in July 1995, the same month it joined ASEAN, and it entered the <b>World Trade Organization</b> in 2007.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Since 2016 Mirziyoyev has opened the economy, making the som convertible, courting foreign companies and reviving a <b>World Trade Organization</b> accession stalled for fifteen years; HSBC counts the country among the 26 fastest-growing economies of the coming decades, and IT services exported about $1 billion in 2024.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
